M3.4: kernel heap (kmalloc) + O(1) double-free guard

- pmm.c: the O(n) double-free scan retires. An in-stack presence
  bitmap (one bit per tracked frame, 4 KiB of .bss) guards pmm_free:
  bit set = the frame sits on the free stack already -> refused.
  Index math is direct since tracked frames are frame-contiguous from
  kernel_limit. alloc clears the bit, free sets it.
- heap.c: bump allocator (16-byte aligned) over the upper half of the
  demand region (DEMAND_BASE + 64 MiB .. +128 MiB, so self-test page
  tables and heap never share PT pages). kmalloc hands out virtual
  addresses WITHOUT eager mapping: pages materialize through the M3.3
  #PF demand pager on first touch - kernel heap and pager are one
  system now. kfree is a documented no-op until userland malloc
  forces a real free-list.
- main.c self-test: 100-byte and 70000-byte (18-page) allocs,
  0xA5/0x5A pattern fills verified byte-for-byte, kmalloc(0) == NULL,
  inter-pointer spacing checked.

